1
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Every 30 seconds of a night's sleep gets a label:
Wake, N1, N2, N3 or REM. Experts score them by hand.

2
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:23,200
Can a computer do it from one EEG channel, a single pair of scalp electrodes?

3
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:37,300
A 2026 paper in Scientific Reports says yes: about 83% accuracy, combining
a synchrosqueezed transform, a CNN, contrastive learning and a GRU.

4
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,100
We set out to reproduce it. Within our time and compute, we could not.

5
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:59,100
So we changed the question. Not “how high can one number go?”
but “does it still work on a person the model has never seen?”

6
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:02,900
And can we explain every step from first principles?

7
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:15,300
Our data is Sleep-EDF, a public PhysioNet set: healthy adults recorded
at home on cassette recorders, usually two nights of about 20 hours.

8
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,600
Trained technicians then scored every 30-second epoch
by hand, following the 1968 Rechtschaffen and Kales rules.

9
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:30,200
We use 20 people and 39 nights: 42,307 epochs. Trimmed
to its scored span, a night is about 1,085 epochs, or 9 hours.

10
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:42,800
The paper chains four ideas. Ours is three plain ones: a
short-time Fourier transform, a CNN and a bidirectional GRU.

11
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:51,600
We dropped the contrastive stage: reproducing it failed within our time
and compute, and we wanted a chain we can draw, derive and check.

12
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:56,400
We make no claim that it helps or hurts: we never ran that comparison.

13
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:09,000
The paper uses a synchrosqueezed transform. We use a plain
STFT: a 2-second Hamming window sliding in 1-second steps.

14
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:16,000
Each 30-second epoch becomes a 129 × 29 picture of
frequency over time. A CNN squeezes it into 128 numbers.

15
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:23,700
A bidirectional GRU then reads 20 neighbouring epochs forwards
and backwards, because sleep is a story, not a snapshot.

16
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:36,200
Testing is strict. Twenty people, ten folds: in each fold 16
people train, 2 choose the checkpoint and 2 are tested.

17
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:42,700
Nobody appears twice. A person's epochs look alike, so splitting by epoch would flatter the score.

18
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:56,200
First result: 56.42% macro-F1, a score that treats all five
stages equally, averaged over people. Accuracy: 65.00%.

19
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:03,600
Then we opened individual nights. On three of them the model
was right on only 7.22%, 18.60% and 19.01% of epochs.

20
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:14,200
Our hypothesis: nights differ in overall signal strength,
so a model trained on some nights misreads others.

21
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:20,500
A per-night yardstick cancels that, at a cost: a night's average also depends on its stage mix.

22
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:31,100
Our idea, R1: judge every night against its own yardstick,
using that night's own mean and spread for each frequency.

23
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:37,600
Same architecture, retrained. Macro-F1 rose from
56.42% to 74.86%; accuracy from 65.00% to 80.99%.

24
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:42,100
The three failing nights recovered to 82.54%, 88.68% and 84.87%.

25
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:53,500
But R1 reads each test night's unlabeled signal within its
scored span, and we chose it after the baseline failed.

26
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:58,600
So it is offline and post-hoc: a promising finding, not a finished product.

27
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:09,200
Our 80.99% sits near the paper's 83%. We do not rank them:
the protocols differ, and R1 sees each test night's signal.

28
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:20,200
A sensitivity check takes the statistics from the whole recording instead: 71.84%.

29
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:28,000
Versus R1: −3.02 points, 95% interval −7.12 to +1.22. It crosses
zero: no clear difference, but not proof they are equal.

30
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:38,200
N1, the lightest sleep, stays hard: R1 recovers 44.69%
of true N1 epochs, and 38.91% of its N1 calls are right.

31
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:41,800
Three small attempts to fix it were all rejected.

32
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:52,000
What we cannot yet say: that it works on new people.
None of 78 audited participants is certified independent.

33
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:55,600
Next: a locked protocol on truly independent data.
